6. Conclusion and outlook
In this paper, we developed a feedforward multilayer network for stimulating the mean exit time that is dominant in stimulating a process to escape a bounded domain. Meanwhile, we devised a machine learning framework to compute the quasipotential and most probable paths of metastable transition events. We proposed an iterative control strategy to steer the mean exit time to reach a desired value. A typical architecture was shown in Fig. 2. This work generated a huge amount of interest. Pragmatically, we illustrated the effectiveness and accuracy of the control strategy with the practical examples about the two-dimensional Maier–Stein system and three-dimensional double-well system.
We can generalize these two-dimensional and three-dimensional cases by further considering the rather cumbersome/difficult cases of general multi-dimensional or time-dependent vector fields. We even suggest a possible treatment in high dimensions by looking for a low-dimensional manifold near which the dynamics are focused. In many variable situations, stochastic neural network can be set up as a type of artificial neural network built by introducing random variations into the network, either by appending the network’s artificial neurons stochastic transfer functions, or by giving them stochastic weights. This makes them become useful tools for optimization problems, since the random fluctuations accelerate the escape of the network from local minima. The expectations are high for future applications in a broad range of disciplines. To be honest, the theories and experiments do not hold for SDE driven by multiplicative noise since the orthogonal decomposition (2.8) of the vector field is only valid for additive noise. We should explore new methods and technologies to handle multiplicative situations.
